const llmActionsProps = {"enabled":true,"providers":["claude","chatgpt","perplexity","gemini"],"placement":"header","copyButton":true,"footerLink":true,"markdownBody":"# Stage 04: A task and a reference {#stage-04-a-task-and-a-reference}\n\nAdd two information types to the guide. *Recording your first track* is a `<task>` with steps that lead to a result. *Supported audio formats* is a `<reference>` with a table of facts.\n\nConcepts explain a subject, tasks describe actions, and references provide information to look up. Each type has a content model that controls its structure. Validation checks that structure; you must also review whether the content serves the topic's purpose.\n\n**Time:** about 20 minutes. **You need:** stage 03 complete.\n\nRecorded output below is an example. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. [Check your work](/dita-tutorial/start-here/run-the-gate) to see the result for your own project.\n\n## Step 1: Write the task {#step-1-write-the-task}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Create `topics/recording-your-first-track.dita`** In the **Explorer**, right-click the `topics` folder, choose **New File**, and enter `recording-your-first-track.dita`. In the **New XML Document** dialog, click **Task**. As in stage 01, the editor sets the `@id` from the file name. The template gives you the task skeleton:\n\n   ```xml\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n\n   <task id=\"recording-your-first-track\">\n     <title>Task Title</title>\n     <shortdesc></shortdesc>\n     <taskbody>\n       <steps>\n         <step>\n           <cmd></cmd>\n         </step>\n       </steps>\n     </taskbody>\n   </task>\n   ```\n\n   Replace the title placeholder (**XML** > **Select Element Content**) and type the short description. Add the `<context>` before `<steps>`, type the first action in the empty `<cmd></cmd>`, then add the `<info>` elements, the other steps, and the `<result>`. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing. The finished task:\n\n   ```xml {title=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"}\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n\n   <task id=\"recording-your-first-track\">\n     <title>Recording your first track</title>\n     <shortdesc>Make your first recording in Audacity: pick a microphone, record, stop and play it back.</shortdesc>\n     <taskbody>\n       <context>\n         <p>This task walks you through making your first audio recording.\n         You need a microphone connected to your computer.</p>\n       </context>\n       <steps>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Open Audacity.</cmd>\n           <info>\n             <p>On first launch Audacity opens an empty project window with a gray waveform area.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the Device Toolbar.</cmd>\n           <info>\n             <p>The Device Toolbar is near the top of the window.\n             The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Record to start recording.</cmd>\n           <info>\n             <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.\n             A flat line means the microphone is not selected correctly: click Stop and check the recording device.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Speak into your microphone, or play the audio you want to capture.</cmd>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Stop when you are finished.</cmd>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Play to listen to your recording.</cmd>\n         </step>\n       </steps>\n       <result>\n         <p>You now have a recorded audio track in your project.\n         The waveform shows the shape of the recorded audio: louder sounds appear as taller waves.</p>\n       </result>\n     </taskbody>\n   </task>\n   ```\n2. **Read the task elements**\n\n   - `<task>` and `<taskbody>` take the place of `<concept>` and `<conbody>`. The DOCTYPE changes with them: `-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN`.\n   - `<context>` says why and when the reader does this task. It comes before the steps and holds ordinary paragraphs.\n   - `<steps>` is the procedure; each `<step>` is one action. The task body allows one `<steps>` (or `<steps-unordered>`, which stage 06 shows).\n   - `<cmd>` is the action itself, and is the one required child of a step. Write it as an imperative sentence. A reader who only reads the `<cmd>` elements should be able to complete the task.\n   - `<info>` is extra information about a step: what to expect, where to find the control. It is optional and sits after the `<cmd>`.\n   - `<result>` says what is true when the steps are done. It comes after the steps.\n3. **Preview the task** Before you preview it, predict how the steps will look to a reader. Then choose **View** > **Preview in Tab**. The steps become a numbered list, one number for each `<cmd>`. The context comes before them, each `<info>` sits under its step, and the result closes the task.\n\n   You wrote structure. How it looks comes from the transform: for the guide, DITA-OT's `html5` transtype, which the deliverable chooses.\n:::\n\n## Step 2: Write the reference {#step-2-write-the-reference}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Create `topics/supported-audio-formats.dita`** Create the file in the `topics` folder in the same way. The template that you used last is now at the top of the list. Click **Reference**:\n\n   ```xml\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN\" \"reference.dtd\">\n\n   <reference id=\"supported-audio-formats\">\n     <title>Reference Title</title>\n     <shortdesc></shortdesc>\n     <refbody>\n       <section>\n         <title>Section Title</title>\n         <p></p>\n       </section>\n     </refbody>\n   </reference>\n   ```\n\n   Replace the title and type the short description. This reference's introductory section has no title, so delete `<title>Section Title</title>`, and type the introduction in the empty `<p></p>`. Then add the `<table>` after the `</section>`. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing. The finished reference:\n\n   ```xml {title=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"}\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN\" \"reference.dtd\">\n\n   <reference id=\"supported-audio-formats\">\n     <title>Supported audio formats</title>\n     <shortdesc>The audio formats Audacity imports and exports, and when to use each one.</shortdesc>\n     <refbody>\n       <section>\n         <p>Audacity imports and exports audio in several formats.\n         Lossless formats keep every sample of the source; lossy formats discard detail to make smaller files.</p>\n       </section>\n       <table>\n         <title>Common audio formats</title>\n         <tgroup cols=\"4\">\n           <colspec colname=\"format\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"type\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"quality\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"notes\" colwidth=\"2*\"/>\n           <thead>\n             <row>\n               <entry>Format</entry>\n               <entry>Type</entry>\n               <entry>Quality</entry>\n               <entry>Notes</entry>\n             </row>\n           </thead>\n           <tbody>\n             <row>\n               <entry>WAV</entry>\n               <entry>Uncompressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossless</entry>\n               <entry>Large files, no quality loss.\n               Best for archiving and further editing.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>MP3</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossy</entry>\n               <entry>Widely supported, small files.\n               Good for sharing and the web.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>OGG Vorbis</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossy</entry>\n               <entry>Open alternative to MP3.\n               Good quality at low bitrates.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>FLAC</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossless</entry>\n               <entry>Smaller than WAV with no quality loss.\n               Ideal for archiving when space matters.</entry>\n             </row>\n           </tbody>\n         </tgroup>\n       </table>\n     </refbody>\n   </reference>\n   ```\n2. **Read the reference elements**\n\n   - `<reference>` and `<refbody>`, with the DOCTYPE `-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN`. A reference body is made of sections, tables and property lists rather than free paragraphs, so the introductory `<p>` sits inside a `<section>`.\n   - `<table>` uses the CALS table model. Use it for row or column spanning, a table title, or detailed column specifications. Its optional `<title>` supplies the table caption. Simple tables also support headers and relative column widths, as stage 05 explains.\n   - `<tgroup cols=\"4\">` declares the number of columns and holds everything else. One table can have several `tgroup`s with different column layouts.\n   - `<colspec>` names each column and sets its width. `colwidth=\"2*\"` is a proportional width: this column gets twice the share of the `1*` columns.\n   - `<thead>` and `<tbody>` hold header and body rows; each `<row>` holds one `<entry>` per column.\n3. **Preview the reference** Before you preview it, predict where the table title will appear and how a reader will tell the header row from the rest. Then choose **View** > **Preview in Tab**. The title is the caption above the table, and the `<thead>` row is the header, set apart from the four body rows. A reader looks up a format by its row, and a fact by its column.\n:::\n\n## Step 3: Update the map and check your work {#step-3-update-the-map-and-check-your-work}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Add the topics to the map** Add a `<topicref>` for each new topic to `audacity-guide.ditamap`, after the existing one, in the order you wrote them: the map lists topics in the order readers meet them. Do this before you check your work: the check reports a topic that no map refers to as an orphan topic. The complete map at this checkpoint is:\n\n   ```xml {title=\"audacity-guide.ditamap\"}\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n   <map>\n     <title>Audio user guide</title>\n     <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n     <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n     <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n   </map>\n   ```\n2. **Format and check your work** Format the map and the topics: choose **XML** > **Format** in each changed file and save it, or choose **Project** > **Project Tools** > **Format Project** to format every file at once. From the command line, run:\n\n   ```bash\n   dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita *.ditamap\n   ```\n\n   Before you check, predict what the navigation of the built guide will show, and in what order. Then choose **Project** > **Check Project** in the editor, or run `dogsbay-xml check .` from the project root. The check rebuilds the guide in `out/full/`. The output looks like this example:\n\n   ```\n   health   clean\n   build    full                 ok  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/out/full\n   output   clean (full)\n   Ready: the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and every link in the pages of full leads somewhere.\n   ```\n\n   `Ready` means that the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and every link in the built pages leads somewhere.\n3. **See the built guide** In the **Explorer**, open `out/full/topics`. The build made a page for each topic. Open `recording-your-first-track.html` and choose **View** > **Preview in Tab**. The navigation lists all three topics in the order of the map, and the task shows its numbered steps, as the preview showed.\n:::\n\nTo see the type split enforced, move the `<p>` in the reference out of its `<section>` so it is a direct child of `<refbody>`, save the file, and predict what the check will say. Then check your work. The reference body does not allow it. A concept body would. The check names the file with the line, column, and message of the error, and stops. The output looks like this example:\n\n```\nhealth   NOT CLEAN\n  invalid: /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n    57:13  The content of element type \"refbody\" does not match its content model.\n  (run project-health for the full report)\nNot ready: the project itself has faults. The build and the built output were not checked.\n```\n\nThe **Project Validation** panel, or `dogsbay-xml project-health .`, gives the full report. For example:\n\n```\nRoot map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nInvalid files (1 of 4):\n  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita:\n    57:13  error: The content of element type \"refbody\" does not match its content model.\n\nSummary\n  Invalid files                 1  of 4\n```\n\nUndo the change, save the file, and check again. Confirm that the check reports `Ready` before you continue.\n\n## What you learned {#what-you-learned}\n\n- A task is `<task>`, `<taskbody>`, `<context>`, `<steps>`, `<step>`, `<cmd>`, `<info>`, `<result>`; the `<cmd>` is the step.\n- A reference is `<reference>`, `<refbody>`, sections and tables; no loose paragraphs.\n- The CALS table: `<table>`, `<tgroup cols>`, `<colspec>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>`, `<row>`, `<entry>`.\n- The three types provide content models for different reader needs.\n\n## Next lesson {#next-lesson}\n\n**Checkpoint:** `tutorial/04-task-and-reference`. If you use Git, commit your work.\n\nContinue with [Stage 05: inline and block](/dita-tutorial/part-1-topics/stage-05-inline-and-block).\n\nFor the core course, use the [learning path](/dita-tutorial/start-here/learning-path) to skip optional modules.\n\n[Compare this stage with its predecessor](https://github.com/dogsbay/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial/compare/tutorial/03-first-build...tutorial/04-task-and-reference).\n","mdUrl":"https://dogsbay.ai/dita-tutorial/part-1-topics/stage-04-task-and-reference.md"} ?? undefined;
