      <h2 id="step-2-write-the-reference" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: Write the reference<a href="#step-2-write-the-reference" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/supported-audio-formats.dita</code> Create the file in the <code>topics</code> folder in the same way. The template that you used last is now at the top of the list. Click <strong>Reference</strong>:
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN\" \"reference.dtd\">\n\n<reference id=\"supported-audio-formats\">\n  <title>Reference Title</title>\n  <shortdesc></shortdesc>\n  <refbody>\n    <section>\n      <title>Section Title</title>\n      <p></p>\n    </section>\n  </refbody>\n</reference>"} lang="xml" />
      <p>Replace the title and type the short description. This reference's introductory section has no title, so delete <code>&lt;title&gt;Section Title&lt;/title&gt;</code>, and type the introduction in the empty <code>&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</code>. Then add the <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> after the <code>&lt;/section&gt;</code>. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing. The finished reference:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN\" \"reference.dtd\">\n\n<reference id=\"supported-audio-formats\">\n  <title>Supported audio formats</title>\n  <shortdesc>The audio formats Audacity imports and exports, and when to use each one.</shortdesc>\n  <refbody>\n    <section>\n      <p>Audacity imports and exports audio in several formats.\n      Lossless formats keep every sample of the source; lossy formats discard detail to make smaller files.</p>\n    </section>\n    <table>\n      <title>Common audio formats</title>\n      <tgroup cols=\"4\">\n        <colspec colname=\"format\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n        <colspec colname=\"type\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n        <colspec colname=\"quality\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n        <colspec colname=\"notes\" colwidth=\"2*\"/>\n        <thead>\n          <row>\n            <entry>Format</entry>\n            <entry>Type</entry>\n            <entry>Quality</entry>\n            <entry>Notes</entry>\n          </row>\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          <row>\n            <entry>WAV</entry>\n            <entry>Uncompressed</entry>\n            <entry>Lossless</entry>\n            <entry>Large files, no quality loss.\n            Best for archiving and further editing.</entry>\n          </row>\n          <row>\n            <entry>MP3</entry>\n            <entry>Compressed</entry>\n            <entry>Lossy</entry>\n            <entry>Widely supported, small files.\n            Good for sharing and the web.</entry>\n          </row>\n          <row>\n            <entry>OGG Vorbis</entry>\n            <entry>Compressed</entry>\n            <entry>Lossy</entry>\n            <entry>Open alternative to MP3.\n            Good quality at low bitrates.</entry>\n          </row>\n          <row>\n            <entry>FLAC</entry>\n            <entry>Compressed</entry>\n            <entry>Lossless</entry>\n            <entry>Smaller than WAV with no quality loss.\n            Ideal for archiving when space matters.</entry>\n          </row>\n        </tbody>\n      </tgroup>\n    </table>\n  </refbody>\n</reference>"} lang="xml" title="topics/supported-audio-formats.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the reference elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;reference&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;refbody&gt;</code>, with the DOCTYPE <code>-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN</code>. A reference body is made of sections, tables and property lists rather than free paragraphs, so the introductory <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> sits inside a <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;table&gt;</code> uses the CALS table model. Use it for row or column spanning, a table title, or detailed column specifications. Its optional <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> supplies the table caption. Simple tables also support headers and relative column widths, as stage 05 explains.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;tgroup cols="4"&gt;</code> declares the number of columns and holds everything else. One table can have several <code>tgroup</code>s with different column layouts.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;colspec&gt;</code> names each column and sets its width. <code>colwidth="2*"</code> is a proportional width: this column gets twice the share of the <code>1*</code> columns.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code> hold header and body rows; each <code>&lt;row&gt;</code> holds one <code>&lt;entry&gt;</code> per column.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Preview the reference</strong> Before you preview it, predict where the table title will appear and how a reader will tell the header row from the rest. Then choose <strong>View</strong> &gt; <strong>Preview in Tab</strong>. The title is the caption above the table, and the <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> row is the header, set apart from the four body rows. A reader looks up a format by its row, and a fact by its column.
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-3-update-the-map-and-check-your-work" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: Update the map and check your work<a href="#step-3-update-the-map-and-check-your-work" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Add the topics to the map</strong> Add a <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> for each new topic to <code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code>, after the existing one, in the order you wrote them: the map lists topics in the order readers meet them. Do this before you check your work: the check reports a topic that no map refers to as an orphan topic. The complete map at this checkpoint is:
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n<map>\n  <title>Audio user guide</title>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n</map>"} lang="xml" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Format and check your work</strong> Format the map and the topics: choose <strong>XML</strong> &gt; <strong>Format</strong> in each changed file and save it, or choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Project Tools</strong> &gt; <strong>Format Project</strong> to format every file at once. From the command line, run:
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita *.ditamap"} lang="bash" />
      <p>Before you check, predict what the navigation of the built guide will show, and in what order. Then choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Check Project</strong> in the editor, or run <code>dogsbay-xml check .</code> from the project root. The check rebuilds the guide in <code>out/full/</code>. The output looks like this example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   clean\nbuild    full                 ok  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/out/full\noutput   clean (full)\nReady: the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and every link in the pages of full leads somewhere."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p><code>Ready</code> means that the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and every link in the built pages leads somewhere.</p>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>See the built guide</strong> In the <strong>Explorer</strong>, open <code>out/full/topics</code>. The build made a page for each topic. Open <code>recording-your-first-track.html</code> and choose <strong>View</strong> &gt; <strong>Preview in Tab</strong>. The navigation lists all three topics in the order of the map, and the task shows its numbered steps, as the preview showed.
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <p>To see the type split enforced, move the <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> in the reference out of its <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> so it is a direct child of <code>&lt;refbody&gt;</code>, save the file, and predict what the check will say. Then check your work. The reference body does not allow it. A concept body would. The check names the file with the line, column, and message of the error, and stops. The output looks like this example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   NOT CLEAN\n  invalid: /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n    57:13  The content of element type \"refbody\" does not match its content model.\n  (run project-health for the full report)\nNot ready: the project itself has faults. The build and the built output were not checked."} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The <strong>Project Validation</strong> panel, or <code>dogsbay-xml project-health .</code>, gives the full report. For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Root map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nInvalid files (1 of 4):\n  /home/you/my-audacity-guide/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita:\n    57:13  error: The content of element type \"refbody\" does not match its content model.\n\nSummary\n  Invalid files                 1  of 4"} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>Undo the change, save the file, and check again. Confirm that the check reports <code>Ready</code> before you continue.</p>
      <h2 id="what-you-learned" class="group scroll-mt-20">What you learned<a href="#what-you-learned" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li>A task is <code>&lt;task&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;taskbody&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;context&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;steps&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;step&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;info&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;result&gt;</code>; the <code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code> is the step.</li>
      <li>A reference is <code>&lt;reference&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;refbody&gt;</code>, sections and tables; no loose paragraphs.</li>
      <li>The CALS table: <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;tgroup cols&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;colspec&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;row&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;entry&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li>The three types provide content models for different reader needs.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="next-lesson" class="group scroll-mt-20">Next lesson<a href="#next-lesson" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
